ntdll,server: POSIX shared memory for a libc that has none.

Three files in Proton's tree call shm_open()/shm_unlink(). Bionic implements
neither, and does not merely omit the symbols -- Android's libc declares the
whole option group missing, in bits/posix_limits.h:

    #define _POSIX_SHARED_MEMORY_OBJECTS __BIONIC_POSIX_FEATURE_MISSING
    /* mmap/munmap are implemented, but shm_open/shm_unlink are not. */

with no stub to link against, so each fails to compile:

    dlls/ntdll/unix/fsync.c:474:   call to undeclared function 'shm_open'
    dlls/ntdll/unix/virtual.c:6610: call to undeclared function 'shm_open'
    server/fsync.c:103:            call to undeclared function 'shm_open'

Two of them are fsync -- Proton's fast synchronisation, the client half and the
wineserver half -- and the third is FEX's per-process statistics region, which
is the same call Vessel once carried a patch for against bylaws' tree. Valve's
tree has that hook too.

A plain file is a complete substitute, because that is all shm_open ever was:
on glibc it opens a path under /dev/shm, which is a tmpfs. Everything the
callers rely on comes from mmap( MAP_SHARED ) on the returned descriptor, and
MAP_SHARED on a regular file is the same shared, coherent mapping. ftruncate,
mmap, close and unlink are untouched, and so is every line of fsync's own logic
-- the callers still build the names they always did.

In a header rather than three copies. include/ is on the include path for both
the server and the ntdll unix half, which are otherwise separate build halves,
and a shim that fsync's two ends must agree about is exactly the thing not to
duplicate.

The new header is registered in include/Makefile.in, which is not optional and
not cosmetic: tools/makedep resolves #include against the files the makefiles
declare, not against the filesystem, so an unlisted header that exists on disk
still fails -- during config.status, with

    dlls/ntdll/unix/fsync.c:53: error: wine/vessel_shm.h: No such file or directory
    config.status: error: could not create Makefile

The directory has to be one every process in the session agrees on and can
write to. XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is that directory and Vessel always sets it (it is in
BOOTSTRAP_SESSION_ENV); TMPDIR and a fixed path follow only so that an unset
variable cannot silently send two processes to two different files -- which for
fsync means a client that cannot find the server's region and calls exit(1).

Guarded on HAVE_SHM_OPEN, which configure already defines through
AC_SEARCH_LIBS(shm_open, rt), so a glibc build gets the real functions and
compiles none of it. `static inline`, so the two of three callers that use only
shm_open raise no unused-function warning.

**Compiling is not working.** This makes fsync buildable; whether it engages on
this device is a separate question and is unmeasured. SessionEnvironment.kt asks
for it by default and deliberately leaves WINEFSYNC out of RESERVED_SESSION_ENV
so a container can set WINEFSYNC=0 and return to server-side waits -- which
matters because fsync does not degrade: fsync_init() ends in exit(1) rather than
falling back. The `fsync` Wine debug channel is offered in Diagnostics for
exactly this: at Errors it reports which of the two failure paths was taken, and
at Everything it is the channel vkd3d-proton's own
programs/vkd3d-fsync-log-to-profile.py parses to show whether D3D12 fence waits
are served by a futex or by a round trip to wineserver.
